For critical status changes and real-time broadcasts, use transactional event handling or an equivalent reliable pattern so database changes and notification delivery do not become irreconcilably inconsistent.

Do not store unnecessary sensitive personal information in logs or audit messages.

## 5.10 Ratings

Support the actual two-way review requirement.

Persist the job, reviewer, review recipient, rating value, optional review text, and timestamps.

Validate rating bounds and participant eligibility.

Prevent unauthorized reviews and duplicate reviews where the business rules require one review per participant per job.

## 5.11 Notifications

Persist relevant notifications so important events are not dependent solely on an open WebSocket connection.

Support read/unread state and references to the relevant job, offer, message, or SOS event where appropriate.

## 5.12 Authentication sessions and security records

Implement persistent refresh-token/session records or an equivalent secure session strategy.

Store refresh tokens in a non-reversible form where applicable. Support rotation, expiry, revocation, and logout.

Add supporting records for idempotency or reliable event processing where necessary.

## 5.13 Schema requirements

Do not create all tables without relationships or constraints.

Use:
- Foreign keys.
- Appropriate unique constraints.
- Indexes on frequently queried ownership and status fields.
- GIST indexes for spatial queries.
- Efficient pagination indexes.
- Database transactions for multi-record state changes.

Create realistic development seed data for customer accounts, workers, service categories, jobs, and offers.

